course: biology
lesson: 273
title: Megalodon and the giants of the sea, past and present
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Megalodon was a giant shark, perhaps 15 to 20 metres long, with teeth up to 17 centimetres, bigger than a child's hand, and it ate whales until it died out about 3.6 million years ago.
explain: Films like to say megalodon still hides in the deep, but a 20-metre shark would leave bitten whales, fresh teeth and sightings everywhere, and none are ever found.
explain: Sharks lose and replace teeth all their lives, thousands of them, so old teeth pile up on the sea floor, and that is why you can find a black fossil shark tooth on a beach.
explain: The deep sea still keeps secrets: the coelacanth, thought extinct for 66 million years, turned up in a fishing net in 1938, and giant squid were first filmed alive only in 2004.
explain: Yet the biggest animal of all time is not a lost monster but alive today: the blue whale, up to about 30 metres long, heavier than any dinosaur.
fact: Megalodon was a giant shark, perhaps 15 to 20 metres long, with teeth up to about 17 centimetres; it ate whales and died out about 3.6 million years ago.
fact: Megalodon is extinct: no fresh teeth, bitten whales or sightings are found; as the seas cooled, its whale prey moved to cold water it could not follow.
fact: Great white sharks competed for the same food, and tooth chemistry suggests they ate at the same level of the food chain, so they may have helped finish megalodon; this is still debated.
fact: Fossil places with many small megalodon teeth near the shore show that the young grew up in warm, shallow nurseries, as great whites and many sharks still do.
fact: Dunkleosteus was an armoured fish that lived about 360 million years ago; it had no teeth but sharp bony blades in its jaws and one of the strongest bites of any fish.
fact: Livyatan was a sperm whale relative with teeth up to 36 centimetres that hunted other whales at the same time as megalodon; it is named after the Leviathan of old stories.
fact: Sea scorpions were relatives of spiders that lived over 400 million years ago, and the biggest were longer than a man.
fact: The coelacanth was thought extinct for 66 million years until one was caught off South Africa in 1938; it lives deep down, and its fleshy fins move like legs.
fact: The blue whale is the biggest animal known ever to have lived, up to about 30 metres long, because water holds up its weight.
fact: Giant squid live deep down in the dark sea, so they are rarely seen; they can be about 12 metres long with their tentacles and were first filmed alive in 2004; the heavier colossal squid was reported to be filmed alive in its deep home in 2025.
fact: Sharks lose and replace thousands of teeth in their lives; old teeth fall to the sea floor, can become fossils, and are turned black by minerals over a long time.
exercise: What was megalodon? | A giant shark | A giant whale | A sea dinosaur | A giant squid
exercise: How long could megalodon teeth be? | About 17 centimetres | About 1 centimetre | About 2 metres long | About 5 millimetres
exercise: What did megalodon eat? | Whales | Only plankton | Seaweed | Only tiny shrimp
exercise: When did megalodon die out? | About 3.6 million years ago | 66 million years ago, with the dinosaurs | About 100 years ago | It is still alive
exercise: Why do we know megalodon is extinct? | No fresh teeth, bitten whales or sightings are ever found | Divers have checked every single cave and trench in the sea | A law was passed by scientists saying that it is extinct | It was seen dying on a beach in 1900, the very last one
exercise: Which shark may have helped finish megalodon? | The great white | The whale shark | The hammerhead | The goldfish
exercise: Where did young megalodons grow up? | In warm, shallow nurseries near the shore | In deep, freezing trenches at the bottom of the sea | In rivers and lakes far inland | Inside their mother's mouth until grown
exercise: What did Dunkleosteus have instead of teeth? | Sharp bony blades in its jaws | A long sucking tube like a straw | A soft beak like a squid's | Nothing, because it had no mouth at all
exercise: When did Dunkleosteus live? | About 360 million years ago | About 3 million years ago | About 3,000 years ago | It lives today
exercise: What was Livyatan? | A sperm whale relative with huge teeth | A giant shark, bigger than megalodon | A sea scorpion as long as a bus | A long-necked plesiosaur with flippers
exercise: What were sea scorpions related to? | Spiders | Fish | Snakes | Octopuses
exercise: What happened to the coelacanth in 1938? | One was caught alive off South Africa | It was declared extinct by scientists | The last one in the world died in a zoo | It was first drawn in a cartoon
exercise: What is the biggest animal known ever to have lived? | The blue whale | Megalodon | Argentinosaurus | The giant squid
exercise: How can a blue whale grow so huge? | Water holds up its weight | It eats rocks to grow heavy | It has no bones to carry | It lives on land and eats grass
exercise: When was a giant squid first filmed alive? | In 2004 | In 1904 | Never | In 1504
exercise: Why are fossil shark teeth on beaches so common? | Sharks lose and replace thousands of teeth | Sharks are made only of teeth and nothing else | Shark teeth float up and wash onto the beach | Every shark swims to the beach to die
exercise: What makes fossil shark teeth black? | Minerals soaking in over a long time | Black paint put on them by fishermen | Sharks eating lumps of coal from the sea floor | The teeth were always black, even in life
test: «saw The Meg, megalodon's still down there in the deep innit» What is right? | No, it is extinct; a shark that big would leave teeth, bitten whales and sightings | Yes, it lives in the Mariana Trench, where it is too deep and dark to be filmed | Yes, a young one is kept in a secret aquarium | No, it slowly turned into the blue whale
test: «megalodon is the biggest animal ever, end of» What is true? | No, the blue whale alive today is bigger | Yes, nothing at all was ever bigger than it | No, a T. rex was bigger and heavier than it | No, a giant squid is bigger
test: Count them: a beach has 4 kids, and each finds 3 shark teeth. How many teeth in all? | 12 | 7 | 4 | 16
test: What would happen to megalodon when the seas cooled and its whale prey moved to cold water? | It could not follow and lost much of its food | It grew a thick coat of fur and followed the whales | It started eating seaweed instead of meat | It crawled out of the sea and lived on land
test: «coelacanth was extinct and then came back to life, that's magic» What is better? | It never died out; it lived deep down where few people looked | Scientists brought it back to life from a fossil in a laboratory | It was a different fish that just had the same name as the old one | It came from space
test: In a cartoon a megalodon chases a submarine through the shallows. Which real fact fits the shallows? | Young megalodons grew up in warm, shallow nurseries | Megalodons never went near shallow water in their lives | Megalodons were born on land and then crawled to sea | Submarines are what megalodons ate
test: «how does a fish bite without teeth lol» How did Dunkleosteus do it? | With sharp bony blades in its jaws | It sucked prey in like a vacuum | It used its tail | It did not bite at all
test: Why did the great white and megalodon probably compete? | They ate at the same level of the food chain | They shared one cave and fought over who slept there | They both ate the same kinds of seaweed | They were the very same species
test: Giant squid tentacles wash up on a beach. Why are giant squid so rarely seen alive? | They live deep down in the dark sea | They live only high up in the mountains | They are invisible to our eyes | They hide in rivers and lakes
test: A cartoon shows a sea scorpion the size of a beetle. What could the real biggest ones be? | Longer than a man | Smaller than an ant | As big as a blue whale | As big as an island
test: «Leviathan's just a monster from an old story» Which real animal is named after it? | Livyatan, a whale with huge teeth | Megalodon | The coelacanth | The giant squid
exam: Identify the evidence that most strongly indicates the extinction of megalodon. | The absence of recent teeth, bitten whale remains and reliable sightings | A decree issued by a scientific society declaring the species to be extinct | The discovery of the final individual, stranded upon a beach in 1900 | The testimony of a cinematic production
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the largest animal known to have existed. | It is the blue whale, which survives at the present day | It is megalodon | It is Argentinosaurus | It is the giant squid
exam: Ascertain the circumstances of the rediscovery of the coelacanth. | A living specimen was taken off South Africa in 1938, the species having been presumed extinct | A frozen specimen was recovered from Siberian permafrost alongside the remains of the mammoth | A fossil was restored to life by scientists by means of preserved material | The species was first described in an ancient legend
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dentition of sharks? | Teeth are shed and replaced continually, thousands being produced over a lifetime | Each shark possesses a single set of teeth which must serve for the whole of its life | Sharks are entirely without teeth | Teeth are replaced only once, in old age
